AUDIO: 'additive friction stir deposition. It\'s a 3D printing technology that my buddy Mark here is going to tell me about. Hey Mark. &gt;&gt; Hey. How are you? &gt;&gt; I\'m great. So, first of all, where are we? &gt;&gt; Uh, so we\'re in Blackburg, Virginia at Virginia Tech, but we\'re not on campus. We\'re off campus at the U Research Group'

[00:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Lab uh where we work with additive friction stir deposition. I\'m a PhD researcher under professor Hong Yu in the material science and engineering department and we are a part of Virginia Tech made uh the Virginia Tech center'

[00:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for additive manufacturing. Friction stir deposition. Yes. &gt;&gt; That is a technique where a metal is uh with pressure and friction made to deposit. &gt;&gt; Yes.'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Okay. Additive means we just put layers to it, right? &gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; Okay. And this is what this machine can do. &gt;&gt; Yes, it is. &gt;&gt; Tell me a little bit about that process. &gt;&gt; Okay. So additive friction stir deposition is a solid state metal additive manufacturing process. So majority of additive manufacturing'

[00:54] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: Joel Telling and Mark standing in front of a large MELD machine.
AUDIO: 'processes involve melting the material. In this process, we never melt it. So we only take it up to about 80% of the melt temperature and we reach the flow stress state of the material where we\'re'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'continually plastically deforming it. So we\'re very closely related to forging metal additive. We can we can use electricity or heat and we we we melt it. Uh lasers hit powder to form it and'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s just typically how it\'s done. This is a solidstate process. &gt;&gt; So we take a solid 1/2 in x2 in bar of material. Absolutely readily available. Uh we order from McMaster car. You can'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'find it anywhere. So it\'s just a generic bar of material and we have very very little restrictions when it comes to feed material. So, as long as it\'ll fit in our feed channel, we could run it through, which gives us a lot of a lot'

[01:44] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: Close-up of Mark speaking, with the MELD machine in the background.
AUDIO: 'of different unique opportunities, which we\'ll talk about later. &gt;&gt; We have a tool. It\'s basically a cylinder that\'s running in the machine, and it has a square channel in it that will key into this bar of material and will feed material through underneath'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the tool. We\'re holding it at a uh large pressure, and we\'re rotating, imparting sheer deformation. And that sheer deformation is what promotes the flow of material and is going to break down any'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'oxides and help metallergically bond that material with prior layers. So this isn\'t going to be a laminated structure. It\'s actually fully dense, fully metallurgically bonded, effectively'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generating a billet part. &gt;&gt; Okay. When we talk about like lasers hitting powder, yes, &gt;&gt; there is a porocity that we have to worry about. A highly porous metal additive part can\'t be qualified for like aircrafts or whatever, right?'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'re fully dense and you\'re not melting it. &gt;&gt; Yes. Absolutely. So, just like you would take say a cast component and then go through a forging process or a hot rolling process to refine all of those defects, get rid of that paracity and'

[02:47] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Joel holding a metal tool, discussing it with Mark in front of the MELD machine.
AUDIO: 'improve the material structure. We have that structure straight off the printer. &gt;&gt; And you you utilize this tool head. &gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; And you said it\'s key. Oh. Oh, cuz this is spinning. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So it\'s spinning and it\'s &gt;&gt; have a circular rod in there. You need'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to It\'s keyed because it\'s &gt;&gt; That makes a lot of sense. So you\'re imparting pressure. &gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; And uh and in part with the pressure and the spinning you\'re getting temperature. &gt;&gt; Yes. So we don\'t actually input energy'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the way that you would with say a laser process or a welding process where you\'re directly using electrical energy or other other electromagnetic energy to melt that material or to deform that'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'material. We\'re purely mechanical. So, think of if you take a paper clip and you bend it back and forth, it gets hot. We\'re doing the same thing here. So, the deformation of the the material is what\'s actually generating the temperature and is maintaining that'

[03:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An animated diagram showing the MELD machine with arrows indicating downward movement.
AUDIO: 'elevated temperature. &gt;&gt; Wait a minute. So, okay, cuz I\' I\'ve done that. I\'ve bent a paperclip over and over. Not to the point of of breaking, but enough to where I can reform it and then it kind of stays in that state. That\'s this.'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yes. Just on a much larger scale. And this is the machine. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; And so, uh, this is huge, first of all. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So, it\'s a big CNC machine, but what\'s different than a typical CNC is'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rather than having a normal small spindle motor, we have a giant spindle motor to be able to transmit a large amount of torque. And we have this big linear actuator that\'s providing the downforce required. So, during this'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'process, uh, our general approximation is we have a F-150 pickup truck at the end of that tool head. So, we can impart up to about 7,000 lb of downforce from that actuator. And when we\'re rotating'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the tool to maintain deposition, especially at high deposition rates, uh we can get up to about 350 lb feet of torque at the print head. [laughter] So, really, really large numbers. Can we see it go?'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely. All right. So, right now we\'re just starting up the G-code. We\'re going to be zinging down the layer height. You can see our position up here. Uh once the spindle kicks on, you\'ll have the torque output the speed it\'s spinning at. You can also see the'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'temperature of the tool itself. And down here you have from the actuator the speed that it\'s moving and the force downward uh continuing that process. So as we Z down into the material, we\'ll hit layer height and we\'ll start'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feeding. And then we\'re going to be waiting there to keep building heat and start flowing material. And once material flows, we\'ll tell the machine to start moving. we\'ve reached enough load to start to compressively deform'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that material and it start to effectively fail. But once you have enough temperature, then it can flow continuously. So rather than breaking off small little pieces, it\'s moving almost like a liquid.'

[05:35] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Closer view of the control panel, focusing on the "Spindle" and "Actuator" graphs with live data.
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; All right. All right. &gt;&gt; Solid the whole time. &gt;&gt; But solid the whole time. &gt;&gt; And that\'s yield. &gt;&gt; Oh, and that\'s yield right there. &gt;&gt; Uh normally around 330C on the readout is when we\'ll start to see the material start to flow. So that\'s looking pretty'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'good. I\'ll tell the machine to move. We\'re now uh forcing material downward at 7 in per minute and traveling at 10 in per minute traverse. &gt;&gt; I see it going. &gt;&gt; Look at that.'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, the temperature should remain fairly stable as well as the torque. So, right now we\'re sitting at about 140 foot-lbs of torque and 1,700 lb of downforce. &gt;&gt; That\'s way less torque than I thought we would need. &gt;&gt; So, it\'s it\'s relatively low depending on the material. This is 6061 aluminum,'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
