AUDIO: 'over the top of that and effectively restore any sort of decrease in performance that they would have from machining that pocket into it using this technology. &gt;&gt; Okay. So if we talk about how they would'

[09:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do it before, you could either place the sensor on right on the surface or you could drill in &gt;&gt; drill in put it inside. Now you have a defect, right? &gt;&gt; And so we can remove that defect. And so we\'re fully enclosing those sensors in'

[09:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the part. We\'re protecting them. we can get better information closer to an area of interest. &gt;&gt; This is Star Trek level stuff. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; So, what we\'ll do is we\'ll take uh some sort of same material enclosure that\'s'

[09:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to cover up over the top of the material, machine a equal kind of pocket in the material, put that over our electronics so it\'ll be flush with the top and we can print over the top of it'

[10:09] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: Joel and Mark continuing their discussion, with the MELD machine in the background.
AUDIO: 'and that\'s going to metalergically bond this puck with that part to minimize that defect. And you can do this with a part that was completely made with AFSD. You can take a prior forging or a prior casting, machine a pocket into it, or'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have that as an original feature, put in a puck, and then print over the top of it. So, you don\'t need it to be an AFSD part. You can take an off-the-shelf existing component that you\'re already producing, and then use this just for'

[10:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sensor embedding process. &gt;&gt; Okay. This is really cool because I see some stuff here, and now we can talk about materials because what we\'ve shown so far is aluminum. &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Okay. But aluminum is not the end- all beall of what you\'re able to do.'

[10:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; No, not at all. So, we can work with a wide variety of materials. So, effectively, if it\'s ductal, we can print it. If you have the force and torque, you can you can print it. And one of the the big requirements is just'

[11:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tooling. So, what we were just running with aluminum is steel tooling. But if you want to deposit steel, all of a sudden, your tool becomes a consumable. Uh so, you\'re required to use a higher temperature material. Typically, it\'s a a lanthnated tungsten.'

[11:13] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Mark standing next to the MELD machine, looking at the printing area.
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Oh, okay. And so with that high temperature tooling that opens up the door to a wide variety of materials. So steels, nickel alloys, a lot of titanium, we\'ve printed copper, uh I know uh nickel, aluminum, bronze has'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'been printed. So a very wide variety of material. &gt;&gt; You just have to make sure that your your your tool melts at a higher temperature &gt;&gt; than the material that you\'re trying to deposit. [laughter] Yeah. &gt;&gt; And and with this being a solid state'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'process, there are a lot of materials that don\'t lend themselves well to meltbased processing. So, we work a lot with 7075 or other 7000 series aluminums that have a high copper content, so they hot crack. So, you need a lot of'

[11:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different additives in your powder to be able to try to print them using other AM techniques, but we can print it off the shelf in the exact alloy as it was intended. &gt;&gt; Oh, so you actually get a more pure part.'

[12:03] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Joel and Mark holding buckets of scrap metal, demonstrating feedstock.
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; Purity in metals isn\'t necessarily something like for being able to qualify a part, that\'s good because you have fully dense, nice, pure materials. But like if I look at stuff like this, this looks like scrap.'

[12:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; It is scrap. So that\'s one really, really cool thing about this process is because it\'s all based on shear deformation, we have a really high feed stock tolerance. So that\'s about 80% dense. And so that is made of these. So'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is twitch. This is &gt;&gt; scrap &gt;&gt; automotive scrap. So whether it be cast parts, stamped sheet, this has pieces of circuit boards in it. &gt;&gt; That was a that was a plug somewhere. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So basically, if you mill that up'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to a small enough size, you press it into the general square shape of a feed bar, throw that up in the machine, and you get back to a fully dense part. &gt;&gt; So this is an 80% dense part, and you\'re'

[12:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saying it still makes a 100% dense. &gt;&gt; Yes. So with that sheer deformation and consolidation, think of again forging. So you take we\'re doing the same thing but taking a bunch of scrap aluminum'

